# scaleup_single_switch_288_3600Gbps.topo
#
# Models an NVLink-class SCALE-UP domain as a single non-blocking crossbar:
# 288 hosts (GPUs) all attached to ONE ToR switch, every pair exactly one hop apart.
# Per-host link = 3600 Gbps (NVLink-aggregate class); switch latency 300 ns, link 500 ns.
#
# HARDWARE CAVEAT: 288 GPUs is a FUTURE / roadmap scale-up size (the mooted "NVL1152" =
# 288 GPU packages, doubling the Rubin-Ultra NVL576). No shipping product puts 288 GPUs
# on a single NVSwitch tier; at this scale the real fabric is a TWO-TIER multi-plane
# NVSwitch network and a single chip's radix (~64) is far exceeded. This single-switch
# model is an IDEALISED upper bound ("fantasise about a very large single domain",
# supervisor 2026-07-13) that isolates the collective's N-scaling on one hypothetical
# non-blocking crossbar. It is deliberately optimistic: it charges neither the second
# switch hop nor any radix/aggregation-context limit. Use for the trend, not as hardware.
#
# Encoding note: htsim's FatTree parser rejects Tiers=1 (fat_tree_topology.cpp), so a
# single switch is expressed as a DEGENERATE 2-tier fat-tree: Podsize == Nodes and
# Tier-0 Radix_Down == Nodes => exactly one ToR holds all hosts. The Tier-1 uplink
# (Radix_Up 1) is vestigial and carries no host<->host traffic (all pairs are same-ToR,
# one hop). Oversubscribed == Nodes keeps radix_down/radix_up == oversub (parser check).
# NOTE: 288 is NOT a power of two, so recursive-doubling is skipped for this N.
Nodes 288
Tiers 2
Podsize 288

Tier 0
Downlink_speed_Gbps 3600
Radix_Down 288
Radix_Up 1
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
Oversubscribed 288

Tier 1
Downlink_speed_Gbps 3600
Radix_Down 1
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
